  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the time required to manufacture a bicycle frame. Then we have $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>52).$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<48)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<48) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ \u03a6(\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & ={\u03a6}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{\u03a6}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 48-\\mu  & =-3.3725 \\\\ \\mu  & =51.3725.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(52<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>52) & =P(Z>\\frac{52-51.3725}{5}) \\\\  & \u2248P(Z>0.13) \\\\  & =1-\u03a6(0.13) \\\\  & =1-0.5517 \\\\  & \u224844.83%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $44.83%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes.",
